  "summary": "A distinctive blue-and-white architectural style originating from the Tunisian village of Sidi Bou Said.",
  "description": "Characterized by white-washed walls with blue doors, windows, and ornamental details, creating a stark contrast against the Mediterranean landscape, inspired by Greek island architecture but with Islamic elements.",
  "culturalContext": "Sidi Bou Said standardized its blue-and-white look in the 1920s when artist and preservationist Rodolphe d'Erlanger had the Tunisian cliff village painted in the now-canonical white lime wash and cobalt blue trim, codifying a Tunisian-Andalusi tradition of door studs, mashrabiya and arched loggias. Tunisian law protects the palette, and the village became North Africa's postcard — an authored vernacular that resorts worldwide imitate.",
